00:00:30.742 --> 00:00:33.077
Hey everybody! 
It's Gary Vay-ner-chuk,

00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:36.902
and this is episode 89
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:39.323
As you can tell, this is only the second,

00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:41.563
is this the second time, Casey, right?

00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:43.722
Only the second time
we've ever had a guest,

00:00:43.722 --> 00:00:47.962
and we've doubled it up
and so I'm really excited.

00:00:47.962 --> 00:00:50.502
As many of you know who watch the show,

00:00:50.502 --> 00:00:54.156
Jack, Suzy and I did a great
talk at South by Southwest,

00:00:54.156 --> 00:00:55.917
I've really gotten to spend
some nice time with them

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:57.237
over the last couple of months,

00:00:57.237 --> 00:00:59.937
obviously have known about
them, known them a little bit,

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:01.777
and got to know them more and more,

00:01:01.777 --> 00:01:03.117
and so this is super fun for me

00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:04.417
for you guys to be on the show.

00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:05.617
I appreciate it.

00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:07.018
- We appreciate being here.

00:01:07.018 --> 00:01:08.412
- Well, why don't you
tell the VaynerNation

00:01:08.412 --> 00:01:10.497
how you've been, what are
you guys up to, who you are

00:01:10.497 --> 00:01:12.357
for the one or two people that don't know.

00:01:12.357 --> 00:01:13.318
Let's do that.

00:01:13.318 --> 00:01:14.278
Suzy, go first.

00:01:14.278 --> 00:01:17.058
Alright, well, we're here cause
we love you so much, Gary,

00:01:17.058 --> 00:01:18.757
but we're here because
we've got our new book out,

00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:20.557
it's called The Real Life MBA,
we're going around talking

00:01:20.557 --> 00:01:22.057
to people about the ideas in it.

00:01:22.057 --> 00:01:24.337
It's an MBA in a book, and you know,

00:01:24.337 --> 00:01:27.757
as background, Jack has got a long history

00:01:27.757 --> 00:01:31.177
in business and I'm his writing
partner and tour partner,

00:01:31.177 --> 00:01:32.897
I've done a lot in business myself.

00:01:32.897 --> 00:01:33.978
- Jack?

00:01:33.978 --> 00:01:37.537
- And I've been building a
school in the last two years,

00:01:37.537 --> 00:01:40.016
the Jack Welch Management Institute.

00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:44.416
We are granting MBAs and we're
growing at 40 percent a year.

00:01:44.416 --> 00:01:48.376
We're up to 900 students,
38 years old average age,

00:01:48.376 --> 00:01:50.536
and it's so much fun to see them progress

00:01:50.536 --> 00:01:51.896
through their companies.

00:01:51.896 --> 00:01:55.176
65 percent of the graduates
have gotten promotions

00:01:55.176 --> 00:01:56.336
while they were there.

00:01:56.336 --> 00:02:00.116
18 percent is the average
raise of the student

00:02:00.116 --> 00:02:01.696
at the school while they're there.

00:02:01.696 --> 00:02:03.416
So it's been a great benefit.

00:02:03.416 --> 00:02:05.037
- And tell them about your back career,

00:02:05.037 --> 00:02:08.116
and I mean you're semi-pro hockey career.

00:02:08.116 --> 00:02:09.196
- [Suzy] Oh yeah.

00:02:09.196 --> 00:02:12.016
- That was a very weak, very weak program.

00:02:12.016 --> 00:02:14.316
Fortunately, I thought
I was a stud when I was

00:02:14.316 --> 00:02:16.776
in high school, and I captained everything

00:02:16.776 --> 00:02:19.596
and won all these awards
and I went to college

00:02:19.596 --> 00:02:22.596
and played against Canadians,
and I quickly realized

00:02:22.596 --> 00:02:24.706
that I stunk. (they laugh)

00:02:24.706 --> 00:02:26.676
- Alright India, let's get into the show.

00:02:26.676 --> 00:02:27.616
We got some questions?

00:02:27.616 --> 00:02:28.376
- [India] We do.

00:02:28.376 --> 00:02:29.556
- Let's do it.

00:02:29.556 --> 00:02:31.056
- [Voiceover] Youssef wants to know,

00:02:31.056 --> 00:02:32.676
"How important is failure?

00:02:32.676 --> 00:02:34.197
"You hear a bunch of people saying

00:02:34.197 --> 00:02:35.776
"how important it is to fail.

00:02:35.776 --> 00:02:37.446
"But is it really?"

00:02:38.536 --> 00:02:40.196
- Youssef, great question.

00:02:40.196 --> 00:02:43.116
You know, look, I think
failure has to be quantified.

00:02:43.116 --> 00:02:46.468
If you fail but you never
can get up from it again,

00:02:46.468 --> 00:02:48.108
you know, that's not a good failure.

00:02:48.108 --> 00:02:50.628
I think failure and adversity are

00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:52.128
the two things I think about.

00:02:52.128 --> 00:02:55.749
For me, as an entrepreneur,
and very entrepreneurial,

00:02:55.749 --> 00:02:58.369
and always in my own
stuff, all the failures

00:02:58.369 --> 00:03:01.608
along the way, even going
back to the baseball card show

00:03:01.608 --> 00:03:04.348
when I was thirteen, that I
paid 400 dollars for a table

00:03:04.348 --> 00:03:06.108
and nobody showed up to
that baseball card show.

00:03:06.108 --> 00:03:07.488
That was a learning lesson.

00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:11.388
Those microfailures were
super, super important.

00:03:11.388 --> 00:03:15.348
I think, you know, it depends
on your stomach, right?

00:03:15.348 --> 00:03:18.929
Like, if you really fail,
like go out of business,

00:03:18.929 --> 00:03:20.788
I think people take
one of two ways, right?

00:03:20.788 --> 00:03:22.749
They're like, just finished,
and they're never able

00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:24.668
to get off the mat, and they
go in a different direction,

00:03:24.668 --> 00:03:27.428
so to me, I think quantifying
the failure's important to me.

00:03:27.428 --> 00:03:28.929
Jack, Suzy?

00:03:28.929 --> 00:03:31.829
- Well, I'll give you
one, I blew up a factory

00:03:31.829 --> 00:03:33.668
the second year I was in business.

00:03:33.668 --> 00:03:36.968
Sky high, my boss all of
a sudden didn't know me,

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:39.388
I went down to see his boss in New York,

00:03:39.388 --> 00:03:42.488
which he pointed me to,
and the guy asked me

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:46.688
a thousand questions,
using the Socratic method.

00:03:46.688 --> 00:03:48.368
And instead of me getting fired,

00:03:48.368 --> 00:03:50.668
which I thought was a high probability,

00:03:50.668 --> 00:03:52.349
I learned something from it.

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:55.088
So, in every one of these
events, you gotta get yourself

00:03:55.088 --> 00:03:57.748
back on the horse, and how
well you get back on the horse

00:03:57.748 --> 00:04:00.989
and how well you ride after
getting knocked on your butt

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:02.868
is a very big deal.

00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:05.268
- We tell our kids,
"Fail early and often."

00:04:05.268 --> 00:04:07.388
Fail, because then what
happens is, when you do fail,

00:04:07.388 --> 00:04:08.988
you realize it doesn't kill you.

00:04:08.988 --> 00:04:10.489
You go on living, and you realize,

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:11.948
"Okay, you know, I can pick myself up,

00:04:11.948 --> 00:04:13.408
"here's how I'm gonna reinvent myself,

00:04:13.408 --> 00:04:14.618
"here's how I'm gonna talk about it,

00:04:14.618 --> 00:04:16.478
"I'm gonna own it, I'm
gonna say I got fired

00:04:16.478 --> 00:04:17.838
"from the Harvard Business Review."

00:04:17.838 --> 00:04:20.188
I tell people I got fired from
the Harvard Business Review,

00:04:20.188 --> 00:04:21.338
and here's the thing.

00:04:21.338 --> 00:04:24.688
It gives you heart for people
who have also fallen down,

00:04:24.688 --> 00:04:26.108
and you actually sort of have a

00:04:26.108 --> 00:04:27.108
- Empathy.

00:04:27.108 --> 00:04:28.512
- A totally different
understanding for people

00:04:28.512 --> 00:04:29.461
who have fallen down.

00:04:29.461 --> 00:04:30.849
You've been there, and you can,

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:32.489
it just makes you a better person.

00:04:32.489 --> 00:04:34.090
You don't wanna keep failing constantly,

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:35.089
but to be scared--

00:04:35.089 --> 00:04:36.279
- That's a very bad idea.

00:04:36.279 --> 00:04:37.779
- No, you don't wanna fail all the time,

00:04:37.779 --> 00:04:39.149
but you can't be too scared of it,

00:04:39.149 --> 00:04:40.129
because it stops you
from doing big things.

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:42.128
- Yeah, you know, it's funny,
I was just having a meeting

00:04:42.128 --> 00:04:43.859
with a lot of senior
executives here at VaynerMedia,

00:04:43.859 --> 00:04:45.319
and I said, "Look, guys.

00:04:45.319 --> 00:04:48.829
"I don't need peacetime generals," right?

00:04:48.829 --> 00:04:52.269
I need wartime generals,
meaning I need executives,

00:04:52.269 --> 00:04:54.349
like of course I know
this brand's going well

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:56.949
and this brand's going well
and this brand's going well.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:04:58.189
What are you doing about the client

00:04:58.189 --> 00:04:59.910
that's not happy right now?

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:03.129
Right? And I think failure
makes people better at that,

00:05:03.129 --> 00:05:05.829
and so that's another kind
of thing that I look for

00:05:05.829 --> 00:05:07.129
in this subject matter.

00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:08.166
Alright, let's move on.

00:05:08.166 --> 00:05:10.773
- [Voiceover] Chris asks,
"Is terminating the bottom

00:05:10.773 --> 00:05:13.158
"10 percent still a good idea?

00:05:13.158 --> 00:05:16.977
"Even on a team of all-stars,
someone has to be last."

00:05:18.447 --> 00:05:20.168
- Why don't you set this
up, because obviously,

00:05:20.168 --> 00:05:21.427
what was the name of that person?

00:05:21.427 --> 00:05:22.307
- [India] Chris.

00:05:22.307 --> 00:05:23.787
- Chris knows about your thesis,

00:05:23.787 --> 00:05:26.547
I mean, this is a very legendary
POV in corporate America,

00:05:26.547 --> 00:05:29.288
entrepreneur land, like this
is one of the iconic things

00:05:29.288 --> 00:05:31.067
you put into the business world.

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:34.487
Really quick, faster than
normal, tell everybody

00:05:34.487 --> 00:05:36.048
what that is so everybody knows,

00:05:36.048 --> 00:05:38.067
and then answer the current version.

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:40.107
Actually, why don't
you come out with that,

00:05:40.107 --> 00:05:41.627
and give it a little
life, and then tell me

00:05:41.627 --> 00:05:43.595
what the 2015 version of it.

00:05:45.065 --> 00:05:47.965
- If you believe that the best team wins

00:05:47.965 --> 00:05:49.785
and that business is a game,

00:05:49.785 --> 00:05:51.925
you've gotta field the best players.

00:05:51.925 --> 00:05:53.866
So in order to field the best players,

00:05:53.866 --> 00:05:56.625
you look at, any one point in time,

00:05:56.625 --> 00:05:59.565
who are your top 20,
who are your middle 70,

00:05:59.565 --> 00:06:01.505
and who are your bottom 10?

00:06:01.505 --> 00:06:04.046
And you wanna take that top 20

00:06:04.046 --> 00:06:06.685
and you wanna make 'em feel six foot four

00:06:06.685 --> 00:06:08.545
and made for power.

00:06:08.545 --> 00:06:10.425
- What if they're six five to begin with?

00:06:10.425 --> 00:06:11.885
- Go to six eight.

00:06:11.885 --> 00:06:13.385
(they laugh)

00:06:13.385 --> 00:06:16.386
And what you wanna do though, desperately,

00:06:16.386 --> 00:06:18.925
is make them know they're that good.

00:06:18.925 --> 00:06:21.323
You want 'em to be excited,
you want 'em turned on,

00:06:21.323 --> 00:06:22.723
you want 'em passionate.

00:06:22.723 --> 00:06:26.244
The middle 70, you want 'em
striving to be in that top 20,

00:06:26.244 --> 00:06:28.843
and the bottom 10, you tell
'em what's wrong with 'em,

00:06:28.843 --> 00:06:31.343
what they're not doing
right, you give 'em a chance,

00:06:31.343 --> 00:06:33.404
if they don't make it, you let 'em go.

00:06:33.404 --> 00:06:37.303
But when you let 'em go, you
love 'em as much on the way out

00:06:37.303 --> 00:06:39.183
as you loved 'em on the way in.

00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:40.323
- I'm a big believer in that part.

00:06:40.323 --> 00:06:41.924
- And that's a big, important thing.

00:06:41.924 --> 00:06:42.983
- I agree.

00:06:42.983 --> 00:06:45.343
Do you feel, now you played at a GE,

00:06:45.343 --> 00:06:47.983
how many employees under
your wing on average?

00:06:47.983 --> 00:06:49.703
- 400,000.

00:06:49.703 --> 00:06:51.983
- Okay, 400,000 employees
that you were in charge of

00:06:51.983 --> 00:06:54.603
during that run as CEO of GE.

00:06:54.603 --> 00:06:57.323
Do you think that thesis holds true

00:06:57.323 --> 00:06:59.543
to VaynerMedia, 500 people?

00:06:59.543 --> 00:07:00.983
- More important.

00:07:00.983 --> 00:07:02.703
The fewer you have.

00:07:02.703 --> 00:07:03.983
- I'm gonna go a little quicker.

00:07:03.983 --> 00:07:06.183
How about, there's a lot
of five people teams here.

00:07:06.183 --> 00:07:08.523
Five to seven people watching, listening--

00:07:08.523 --> 00:07:11.443
- And it makes it harder for some,

00:07:11.443 --> 00:07:14.424
because you're looking straight at

00:07:14.424 --> 00:07:16.863
the five people who got you started.

00:07:16.863 --> 00:07:18.543
And it's so tough.

00:07:18.543 --> 00:07:20.583
I was talking to somebody last night

00:07:20.583 --> 00:07:24.683
at a conference who said he had to let go

00:07:24.683 --> 00:07:28.263
his former junior partner,
and it was killing him.

00:07:28.263 --> 00:07:31.143
But he had to do it,
she wasn't delivering.

00:07:31.143 --> 00:07:33.243
And I said, "Look, as long
as you take care of her

00:07:33.243 --> 00:07:35.143
"and you're fair, and
you've explained to her

00:07:35.143 --> 00:07:37.583
"for the last year what
she wasn't doing right,

00:07:37.583 --> 00:07:38.763
"you gotta do it."

00:07:38.763 --> 00:07:39.763
- Do you think--

00:07:39.763 --> 00:07:41.513
- The thing that gets me
about this question is,

00:07:41.513 --> 00:07:42.872
the thing we hear again and again,

00:07:42.872 --> 00:07:45.131
we hear in this question
is, even if everybody,

00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:46.271
even if it's an all-star team.

00:07:46.271 --> 00:07:47.271
- No such thing.

00:07:47.271 --> 00:07:48.672
- And there's no such thing.

00:07:48.672 --> 00:07:50.251
You've gotta look at your team and say,

00:07:50.251 --> 00:07:51.791
"Who is the weakest player, can I upgrade,

00:07:51.791 --> 00:07:53.031
"can I upgrade?"

00:07:53.031 --> 00:07:54.212
- It's never existed.

00:07:54.212 --> 00:07:55.971
- It's very, very, I mean,
you can't fall in love

00:07:55.971 --> 00:07:57.991
with your team and say, "Now
I have the perfect team."

00:07:57.991 --> 00:07:59.551
But the next manager comes in and says,

00:07:59.551 --> 00:08:01.071
"What was that person thinking?"

00:08:01.071 --> 00:08:02.271
- Yeah, I mean--

00:08:02.271 --> 00:08:03.971
- I have another take on this, too.

00:08:03.971 --> 00:08:06.351
Why do we always focus on the bottom 10

00:08:06.351 --> 00:08:08.111
in this 20-70-10?

00:08:08.111 --> 00:08:11.511
Why aren't we saying,
"How exciting the top 20!"

00:08:11.511 --> 00:08:12.671
- 100 percent.

00:08:12.671 --> 00:08:14.031
- Why aren't we talking about the winners?

00:08:14.031 --> 00:08:15.311
- Because it's rubbernecking.

00:08:15.311 --> 00:08:16.791
It's the same reason
that we sit in traffic

00:08:16.791 --> 00:08:18.071
all the time, Jack.

00:08:18.071 --> 00:08:19.831
Alright, let's move on.

00:08:19.831 --> 00:08:21.952
- [Voiceover] Jeremy
asks, "As businesses grow,

00:08:21.952 --> 00:08:25.272
"what is the best solution for
documenting policy, procedure

00:08:25.272 --> 00:08:28.541
"and process so all are on the same page?"

00:08:30.091 --> 00:08:32.871
- Jeremy, I hate this question
for a couple of reasons.

00:08:32.871 --> 00:08:34.390
You know, it's interesting.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:36.251
I'm gonna piggy-back off
the last statement here,

00:08:36.251 --> 00:08:39.812
which is, to me, this is
a defense question, right?

00:08:39.812 --> 00:08:42.270
This is a bottom 10 percent question.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:43.948
- Yeah.

00:08:43.948 --> 00:08:47.629
- There is no business on earth that won

00:08:47.629 --> 00:08:51.729
because they had a tight
handbook situation, right?

00:08:51.729 --> 00:08:55.589
So, for me, I mean, unless
you're talking about liabilities

00:08:55.589 --> 00:08:57.089
on a legal level, you know.

00:08:57.089 --> 00:08:59.550
GE should worry about that to some degree

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.289
because of the level of lawsuits.

00:09:01.289 --> 00:09:03.549
and by the way, they
have 400,000 employees,

00:09:03.549 --> 00:09:06.249
and there's probably 8,000
lawyers, there's people to do it,

00:09:06.249 --> 00:09:08.169
but a company of 500 people.

00:09:08.169 --> 00:09:10.749
India, they're not gonna hear
this, but you prepped them,

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:12.209
like how we have a handbook here

00:09:12.209 --> 00:09:13.889
and nobody really knows about it.

00:09:13.889 --> 00:09:16.349
I mean, this is a 500 person,
and it's still a baby,

00:09:16.349 --> 00:09:18.169
like that is something
that I think you need

00:09:18.169 --> 00:09:19.730
to be worrying about at your--

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:22.569
- Yeah, forget documentation,
have a culture.

00:09:22.569 --> 00:09:24.290
Over here has a culture.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.813
And as soon as you're documenting things,

00:09:25.813 --> 00:09:27.913
you're wasting everyone's
time, it becomes a bureaucracy,

00:09:27.913 --> 00:09:29.813
all that matters are your
values and your culture.

00:09:29.813 --> 00:09:30.913
Make sure they're being lived,

00:09:30.913 --> 00:09:32.233
and you don't have to document anything.

00:09:32.233 --> 00:09:34.573
- But everybody has to
know where you're going,

00:09:34.573 --> 00:09:37.333
why you're going there, and
how you're gonna get there.

00:09:37.333 --> 00:09:38.673
- Well, that's leadership, right?

00:09:38.673 --> 00:09:40.593
- And that is the job that--

00:09:40.593 --> 00:09:42.053
- That's my job.

00:09:42.053 --> 00:09:44.153
- That's yours, and the
people that work for you,

00:09:44.153 --> 00:09:45.753
and cascading down.

00:09:45.753 --> 00:09:48.313
But everybody's gotta know the mission,

00:09:48.313 --> 00:09:50.814
they gotta know how they're
gonna do it with behaviors,

00:09:50.814 --> 00:09:52.973
and then they've gotta be, and what are

00:09:52.973 --> 00:09:54.653
the consequences of getting there?

00:09:54.653 --> 00:09:55.993
- There's another thing that I think

00:09:55.993 --> 00:09:57.433
people need to understand.

00:09:57.433 --> 00:09:59.333
And by the way, this is
gonna get very Vaynerized,

00:09:59.333 --> 00:10:00.753
and India, you'll enjoy this.

00:10:00.753 --> 00:10:01.841
And everybody here.

00:10:02.811 --> 00:10:04.931
As I started bringing
in more senior people,

00:10:04.931 --> 00:10:06.831
they wanted to bring in
more of these things.

00:10:06.831 --> 00:10:08.231
And I made them understand, I'm like,

00:10:08.231 --> 00:10:09.492
"Look, you don't understand.

00:10:09.492 --> 00:10:11.951
"We're still this entrepreneurial engine."

00:10:11.951 --> 00:10:13.971
And if somebody comes into this company

00:10:13.971 --> 00:10:16.172
and they're so worried about the handbook,

00:10:16.172 --> 00:10:18.731
and so worried about
reviews, and so worried

00:10:18.731 --> 00:10:20.931
about all these things, I
don't want them here now.

00:10:20.931 --> 00:10:22.772
Not that they're bad, but they're not

00:10:22.772 --> 00:10:24.971
the right players at this time.

00:10:24.971 --> 00:10:28.431
I was the best player on my
fourth-grade baseball team.

00:10:28.431 --> 00:10:30.511
There's not a single
baseball team in America

00:10:30.511 --> 00:10:32.771
in Major League baseball
that needs me on their team.

00:10:32.771 --> 00:10:34.751
So, I was the right player for that time,

00:10:34.751 --> 00:10:36.671
but then as everybody
got much bigger than me,

00:10:36.671 --> 00:10:38.151
I became not so much.

00:10:38.151 --> 00:10:40.291
And so we now need different people

00:10:40.291 --> 00:10:42.071
that maybe care about some of those things

00:10:42.071 --> 00:10:44.571
as we continue to grow,
but not at that time.

00:10:44.571 --> 00:10:46.611
So the other thing here
is, the right employee

00:10:46.611 --> 00:10:50.050
at the right time, at the
right age of the company.

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.329
- [India] That's really good.

00:10:51.329 --> 00:10:52.349
- Thanks, India.

00:10:52.349 --> 00:10:53.429
(they laugh)

00:10:53.429 --> 00:10:54.649
- Yay, boss.

00:10:54.649 --> 00:10:56.089
- [India] That's just beginning material.

00:10:56.089 --> 00:10:58.069
- No, no, she, you know what's so funny,

00:10:58.069 --> 00:10:59.305
back to what we talked about.

00:10:59.305 --> 00:11:00.545
She wasn't "yay, boss-ing"
me, she was like,

00:11:00.545 --> 00:11:01.805
"Oh crap, cool,

00:11:01.805 --> 00:11:03.225
"I have a new article that I can write.

00:11:03.225 --> 00:11:04.561
"He finally said something
different for the first time."

00:11:04.561 --> 00:11:05.931
(they laugh)

00:11:06.841 --> 00:11:08.701
- [Voiceover] Jared wants to
know, "What's the best way

00:11:08.701 --> 00:11:12.411
"to scale a business with an
inherently low profit margin?"

00:11:14.421 --> 00:11:17.941
- So, Jared's got a low
profit margin issue.

00:11:17.941 --> 00:11:20.301
He's asking the best way to scale it.

00:11:20.301 --> 00:11:21.661
Jack?

00:11:21.661 --> 00:11:25.741
- New products, new innovation, new angle.

00:11:25.741 --> 00:11:28.061
If you've got, the last thing
you wanna be thinking about

00:11:28.061 --> 00:11:32.921
is making large something
that's small margins.

00:11:32.921 --> 00:11:36.401
You wanna find ways to
take the assets you have

00:11:36.401 --> 00:11:40.461
and transform it into a
high-growth, exciting player

00:11:40.461 --> 00:11:42.781
in maybe adjacent fields.

00:11:42.781 --> 00:11:45.941
But take the asset you
have and deploy them

00:11:45.941 --> 00:11:47.901
to grow the business, but not maybe

00:11:47.901 --> 00:11:49.901
the existing business completely.

00:11:49.901 --> 00:11:51.741
- Get away from the low-margin businesses.

00:11:51.741 --> 00:11:53.241
Create services, right?

00:11:53.241 --> 00:11:54.541
I mean, isn't that what you did at GE?

00:11:54.541 --> 00:11:56.061
Took all the stuff that was low-margin,

00:11:56.061 --> 00:11:57.921
and you added on high-margin services.

00:11:57.921 --> 00:11:59.941
You don't wanna be in
a low-margin business.

00:11:59.941 --> 00:12:01.901
You've gotta do everything
with your innovation

00:12:01.901 --> 00:12:04.241
and creativity to get away from it.

00:12:04.241 --> 00:12:05.942
- I'm gonna go with an
interesting, different angle

00:12:05.942 --> 00:12:07.501
from my own life experience.

00:12:07.501 --> 00:12:08.961
When I got into my dad's business,

00:12:08.961 --> 00:12:10.582
it did three million dollars in revenue

00:12:10.582 --> 00:12:14.021
and ran on 10 percent gross
profit before expenses.

00:12:14.021 --> 00:12:15.581
It was a family business, right?

00:12:15.581 --> 00:12:16.881
- Right, right.

00:12:16.881 --> 00:12:18.021
- That's low margin.

00:12:18.021 --> 00:12:19.921
The liquor business is bad,
cause there's a middle,

00:12:19.921 --> 00:12:21.881
wholesale part that takes 25 percent

00:12:21.881 --> 00:12:24.201
of the 50 percent that a
retailer normally takes.

00:12:24.201 --> 00:12:26.021
Extremely low margin.

00:12:26.021 --> 00:12:28.322
What I did was actually
went the other way.

00:12:28.322 --> 00:12:30.761
Meaning, I took the low margin items

00:12:30.761 --> 00:12:32.781
that were driving the store's business,

00:12:32.781 --> 00:12:34.442
and I actually bet on them.

00:12:34.442 --> 00:12:37.421
What I did was, I took all the
items like Santa Margherita,

00:12:37.421 --> 00:12:39.521
Kendall-Jackson, the liquor
items that were low margin,

00:12:39.521 --> 00:12:42.441
at cost, by the way, and
I used them to market,

00:12:42.441 --> 00:12:45.722
to drive people into
the store as the honey,

00:12:45.722 --> 00:12:48.182
and then I merchandized the store

00:12:48.182 --> 00:12:51.381
and built the brand that they
came in for, Kendall-Jackson,

00:12:51.381 --> 00:12:53.921
but I sold them a different
Chardonnay with margin.

00:12:53.921 --> 00:12:56.521
And so, both these things
are, of course, right,

00:12:56.521 --> 00:12:58.161
I'm curious, and we don't know,

00:12:58.161 --> 00:12:59.781
where you are in your life cycle.

00:12:59.781 --> 00:13:02.562
What I've watched a lot
of people do is try to go,

00:13:02.562 --> 00:13:04.981
you know, bandaid off from low margin

00:13:04.981 --> 00:13:07.621
to some new innovation that
maybe the market doesn't want.

00:13:07.621 --> 00:13:09.981
I think the hedge there, that's
interesting to understand,

00:13:09.981 --> 00:13:13.881
I really felt the effects of
this, is use it as an offense.

00:13:13.881 --> 00:13:16.381
Right now, we're testing Facebook ads

00:13:16.381 --> 00:13:18.441
for my wine business, and
everything we're using

00:13:18.441 --> 00:13:20.101
are these low-margin
items, because they have

00:13:20.101 --> 00:13:22.821
the brand equity to drive in,
and then we'll take care of it

00:13:22.821 --> 00:13:24.941
from there, so you may be far enough along

00:13:24.941 --> 00:13:26.951
in your business where,

00:13:28.101 --> 00:13:30.121
the first answers here
were 100 percent right.

00:13:30.121 --> 00:13:31.561
You need to get into a
place where the margins

00:13:31.561 --> 00:13:33.801
are gonna get right,
but I worry, depending

00:13:33.801 --> 00:13:35.601
on your sophistication as an operator,

00:13:35.601 --> 00:13:37.141
that you go complete cold turkey.

00:13:37.141 --> 00:13:39.282
There's a way to use the
low-margin items as an asset.

00:13:39.282 --> 00:13:42.241
- That's a very good idea, but I would use

00:13:42.241 --> 00:13:44.721
the low-margin items and the margin

00:13:44.721 --> 00:13:48.201
from those low-margin items
to invest in other areas.

00:13:48.201 --> 00:13:49.421
- 100 percent.

00:13:49.421 --> 00:13:50.781
That's what I did, right?

00:13:50.781 --> 00:13:53.021
The pennies we made turned
into the advertising,

00:13:53.021 --> 00:13:56.481
that drove people in, then I
grabbed them, and so, da-da-da.

00:13:56.481 --> 00:13:57.671
India.

00:13:58.581 --> 00:14:00.601
- [Voiceover] West Coast
Beer Geek wants to know,

00:14:00.601 --> 00:14:04.131
"How can efficiency and
creativity better work together?"

00:14:05.881 --> 00:14:07.521
- They will be inefficient into the time

00:14:07.521 --> 00:14:09.201
and space you give them, to be honest.

00:14:09.201 --> 00:14:11.621
I mean, I was a creative a long time,

00:14:11.621 --> 00:14:13.361
and then I became the
boss of the creatives,

00:14:13.361 --> 00:14:16.501
and I knew how much fat was
built into their writer's block

00:14:16.501 --> 00:14:19.941
and their, you know, thinking
and everything, and--

00:14:19.941 --> 00:14:21.522
- The zen room they needed.

00:14:21.522 --> 00:14:23.521
- And I had to come out
of the associate press,

00:14:23.521 --> 00:14:26.402
where you had to turn out
seven, eight stories a day.

00:14:26.402 --> 00:14:29.301
I knew that the creativity
expanded into that space.

00:14:29.301 --> 00:14:30.961
And of course, you gotta be creative,

00:14:30.961 --> 00:14:33.761
and creatives need some time
to decompress and so forth,

00:14:33.761 --> 00:14:36.121
but we give them a little bit too much,

00:14:36.121 --> 00:14:39.201
or maybe not we in general, but it is easy

00:14:39.201 --> 00:14:41.542
to listen to them moan and
groan about needing more,

00:14:41.542 --> 00:14:43.681
and so creatives can be efficient.

00:14:43.681 --> 00:14:46.481
- To get efficiency,
you gotta be creative,

00:14:46.481 --> 00:14:48.461
and you gotta have creative people.

00:14:48.461 --> 00:14:52.101
And efficiency doesn't
mean some guy or woman

00:14:52.101 --> 00:14:54.561
in a hole, driving every day.

00:14:54.561 --> 00:14:56.541
It means that people
are thinking of new ways

00:14:56.541 --> 00:14:59.281
to do things, coming up with
incremental improvements,

00:14:59.281 --> 00:15:01.281
making things better every day.

00:15:01.281 --> 00:15:06.121
Finding a better way every
day takes a thinking head set,

00:15:06.121 --> 00:15:08.901
and you want that
mentality in your company.

00:15:08.901 --> 00:15:12.642
You want the whole company
to be thinking about,

00:15:12.642 --> 00:15:17.012
every day, what is a better way
of doing what I'm doing now?

00:15:17.012 --> 00:15:19.581
- I'm a big fan of
betting on your strengths,

00:15:19.581 --> 00:15:23.962
and also really
recognizing putting players

00:15:23.962 --> 00:15:26.021
in the best position to succeed.

00:15:26.021 --> 00:15:27.921
And we have nothing but creatives here,

00:15:27.921 --> 00:15:30.061
of the 500 employees, 200 of them,

00:15:30.061 --> 00:15:32.121
and if I've deemed, if we've deemed,

00:15:32.121 --> 00:15:34.301
if Tina, who runs our
Creative Department deems

00:15:34.301 --> 00:15:36.421
that this person is bringing us quality,

00:15:36.421 --> 00:15:38.161
I think one thing that a
lot of people try to do

00:15:38.161 --> 00:15:39.881
is mold them into being more efficient.

00:15:39.881 --> 00:15:42.041
I've done that plenty
of times in my career.

00:15:42.041 --> 00:15:43.941
One of the things I've
decided now is to look at it

00:15:43.941 --> 00:15:46.322
more as a net-net game, right?

00:15:46.322 --> 00:15:48.101
You know, I may not like
that they need to be

00:15:48.101 --> 00:15:49.981
in a zen room with unicorns in it,

00:15:49.981 --> 00:15:52.321
I may not, but if I'm
okay with the output.

00:15:52.321 --> 00:15:55.961
If I'm okay, net-net, 365 day year output,

00:15:55.961 --> 00:15:57.381
I'll take it, right?

00:15:57.381 --> 00:15:59.521
You could have the most
prima donna creative,

00:15:59.521 --> 00:16:01.481
but if they do that one
thing that you decide

00:16:01.481 --> 00:16:03.961
drives the ROI, on the flip
side, you could have somebody

00:16:03.961 --> 00:16:06.561
who's the most efficient
but lacks the magic.

00:16:06.561 --> 00:16:08.482
What you have to really do is,

00:16:08.482 --> 00:16:10.421
it's wide receivers in football.

00:16:10.421 --> 00:16:12.721
Listen, they're at the
mercy of the quarterback

00:16:12.721 --> 00:16:14.241
getting them the ball, that's why,

00:16:14.241 --> 00:16:15.721
they don't get to touch the ball.

00:16:15.721 --> 00:16:16.901
The quarterback touches the ball,

00:16:16.901 --> 00:16:18.921
the running back touches the
ball when the call's played,

00:16:18.921 --> 00:16:20.681
the receivers don't, so many variables,

00:16:20.681 --> 00:16:23.121
and I'm very intrigued by that psychology.

00:16:23.121 --> 00:16:27.201
That being said, you know, I value speed

00:16:27.201 --> 00:16:29.381
and execution over everything.

00:16:29.381 --> 00:16:31.761
And so, I definitely sit
on that Mendoza line,

00:16:31.761 --> 00:16:33.222
if there's a coin toss.

00:16:33.222 --> 00:16:35.761
If I'm even debating it, if
I'm even debating your value

00:16:35.761 --> 00:16:38.401
as a creative over the
efficiency and the output,

00:16:38.401 --> 00:16:40.251
you're in trouble.

00:16:41.741 --> 00:16:44.641
- That's sensational, one of the things.

00:16:44.641 --> 00:16:45.981
No it is!
- Thank you, Jack.

00:16:45.981 --> 00:16:49.401
- One of the things that
really can kill a company

00:16:49.401 --> 00:16:52.781
is the innovators sit over here in a box,

00:16:52.781 --> 00:16:56.301
and they are Thomas Edison,
and they are Steve Jobs,

00:16:56.301 --> 00:16:57.361
- The ninjas.

00:16:57.361 --> 00:16:58.841
- And they're these people.

00:16:58.841 --> 00:17:00.821
And then, everybody
else, keep your head down

00:17:00.821 --> 00:17:02.282
and be a grunt.

00:17:02.282 --> 00:17:04.201
You lose the minds of these people.

00:17:04.201 --> 00:17:07.501
You want everybody to be an innovator!

00:17:07.501 --> 00:17:08.911
- Right.
- 100 percent.

00:17:08.911 --> 00:17:10.535
And it's interesting, here at Vayner,

00:17:10.535 --> 00:17:13.800
we're a classic agency,
we want more practicality

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:16.221
from our creatives, and we
want our account strategists

00:17:16.221 --> 00:17:18.501
being creative, and that's
been a big benefit for us.

00:17:18.501 --> 00:17:21.060
And, you know what else it
does, it creates mutual respect.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.540
Because when the innovators are over here,

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:27.461
they sit on a higher ground,
and it deflates the momentum

00:17:27.461 --> 00:17:28.841
and the equity.

00:17:28.841 --> 00:17:31.301
- Right, any time you get prima
donnas in an organization,

00:17:31.301 --> 00:17:32.821
it enervates everyone around.

00:17:32.821 --> 00:17:34.541
- So, let's wrap up with this, we don't do

00:17:34.541 --> 00:17:36.781
a wrap-up session, but we're
gonna make a unique thing.

00:17:36.781 --> 00:17:38.882
Why this book, and more importantly,

00:17:38.882 --> 00:17:40.421
you have such a mix of people that listen

00:17:40.421 --> 00:17:42.121
and watch my show, right?

00:17:42.121 --> 00:17:44.281
You've got a ton of people in startup,

00:17:44.281 --> 00:17:46.501
entrepreneurial culture,
you know, obviously

00:17:46.501 --> 00:17:48.321
cause of the agency,
there's a plenty amount

00:17:48.321 --> 00:17:52.181
of executives in Fortune
500s, bloggers, media types.

00:17:52.181 --> 00:17:54.381
When you look at this book,

00:17:54.381 --> 00:17:57.122
and you guys put your
self-awareness against this book,

00:17:57.122 --> 00:17:58.601
who benefits the most?

00:17:58.601 --> 00:18:00.401
Of course, that's like wanting
the book to be successful.

00:18:00.401 --> 00:18:02.321
We want everybody, and I'd
like, it would mean a lot to me

00:18:02.321 --> 00:18:04.281
to support these guys, I want you on it,

00:18:04.281 --> 00:18:06.241
but going down the pecking order,

00:18:06.241 --> 00:18:09.022
who do you think, or how
do the individual segments

00:18:09.022 --> 00:18:11.441
that could read this book, what
do you see them benefitting

00:18:11.441 --> 00:18:12.981
from what angles?

00:18:12.981 --> 00:18:15.061
- I mean, what we wanted to
do, we wanted to create an MBA

00:18:15.061 --> 00:18:17.341
in a book, because you
can start off in business,

00:18:17.341 --> 00:18:19.081
and you can get very good at one thing,

00:18:19.081 --> 00:18:20.861
and there's something in your
head that's saying to you,

00:18:20.861 --> 00:18:23.122
I wish I had the 360 mindset, you know,

00:18:23.122 --> 00:18:25.221
that's sort of what an MBA,
I've got one, what it gives you.

00:18:25.221 --> 00:18:27.161
It sort of gives you the CEO's mindset,

00:18:27.161 --> 00:18:29.021
you sort of see all
the different functions

00:18:29.021 --> 00:18:31.161
working together, and we looked around,

00:18:31.161 --> 00:18:32.661
and that book didn't exist.

00:18:32.661 --> 00:18:34.542
It's for people who didn't have an MBA

00:18:34.542 --> 00:18:36.141
who just wanted one but
didn't wanna give up

00:18:36.141 --> 00:18:37.361
two years of their life to go get one.

00:18:37.361 --> 00:18:39.201
- Or the gadrillion dollars, or the debt.

00:18:39.201 --> 00:18:41.891
- Right, the flexibility, everything.

00:18:41.891 --> 00:18:44.857
- Well, I mean, anybody
who's managing anyone,

00:18:44.857 --> 00:18:48.837
a small team of five
people or a team of 500,

00:18:48.837 --> 00:18:51.437
can use the tools that are in here

00:18:51.437 --> 00:18:53.217
to build a team.
- So, management.

00:18:53.217 --> 00:18:55.838
- No, no, no, that's one level.

00:18:55.838 --> 00:18:57.957
- I know, but that, but
it's an interesting insight

00:18:57.957 --> 00:19:00.297
that that's where you went
first, but I'm intrigued by it.

00:19:00.297 --> 00:19:01.267
- Well, I--

00:19:01.267 --> 00:19:02.478
- He always goes there first (laughs)
because he--

00:19:02.478 --> 00:19:04.528
- I believe in it so
much, so I'm into it too.

00:19:04.528 --> 00:19:06.807
- But I'm talking about
managing small groups as well.

00:19:06.807 --> 00:19:07.888
- I get it.

00:19:07.888 --> 00:19:12.457
- And what we wanted to
do is create an atmosphere

00:19:12.457 --> 00:19:15.497
in a company that's got some
of the buzz you've got here,

00:19:15.497 --> 00:19:17.077
across every company.

00:19:17.077 --> 00:19:21.178
So, building a wow team,
and having employees know

00:19:21.178 --> 00:19:25.037
how to play on a wow team is
a critical part of this story.

00:19:25.037 --> 00:19:27.717
- One last thing is that there's
a whole section of the book

00:19:27.717 --> 00:19:29.697
on your career, and it's about, you know,

00:19:29.697 --> 00:19:31.017
what should I do with my life,

00:19:31.017 --> 00:19:32.677
how do I get out of a career stall,

00:19:32.677 --> 00:19:34.417
and how do you become an entrepreneur,

00:19:34.417 --> 00:19:35.857
what does it really take?

00:19:35.857 --> 00:19:37.437
There's all, a lot of the book is

00:19:37.437 --> 00:19:40.037
about winning strategically,
smiting your competition,

00:19:40.037 --> 00:19:41.743
building a great team, but look frankly,

00:19:41.743 --> 00:19:43.353
people spend a lot of brain time thinking

00:19:43.353 --> 00:19:44.913
about their own careers and how they get

00:19:44.913 --> 00:19:46.053
to be where

00:19:46.053 --> 00:19:47.173
- Stuck.

00:19:47.173 --> 00:19:48.614
- Where they're really
fulfilled and they're doing

00:19:48.614 --> 00:19:49.713
what they wanna do, so the whole,

00:19:49.713 --> 00:19:51.333
there's a chunk of the book about that.

00:19:51.333 --> 00:19:53.973
- Do you believe, on a real
quick question, do you believe,

00:19:53.973 --> 00:19:55.833
by the way, you guys
look great in this photo.

00:19:55.833 --> 00:19:57.593
Do you believe, how many photos,

00:19:57.593 --> 00:19:59.133
how long did this photo shoot take?

00:19:59.133 --> 00:20:01.354
- Long time for me,

